import{_ as t,o as a,c as i,a0 as r}from"./chunks/framework.TJhnt8t4.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Threat model","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guide/threat-model.md","filePath":"en/guide/threat-model.md"}'),o={name:"en/guide/threat-model.md"};function n(s,e,c,l,h,d){return a(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="threat-model" tabindex="-1">Threat model <a class="header-anchor" href="#threat-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Threat model&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Every time a mobile app sends a request to an API, it does so in an environment the developer does not control. The user might be on an airport&#39;s public Wi-Fi, routing traffic through a corporate proxy that terminates HTTPS, or running a rooted device with a third-party certificate installed. In each of these scenarios, the app&#39;s traffic is potentially exposed to third parties — this is the standard threat model for any client-server application, not an edge case.</p><p>HTTPS is widely considered a solved problem for secure data transmission. That&#39;s only partially true: HTTPS guarantees encryption and server authenticity, but only within the trust boundaries of the public CA infrastructure — and that infrastructure is broader than most developers realize. The default root store on an Android device trusts over 130 CAs. Any one of them can issue a valid certificate for your domain. If even one is compromised — or a user has installed a rogue root certificate, intentionally or not — a connection that appears secure is anything but.</p><h2 id="attack-vectors" tabindex="-1">Attack vectors <a class="header-anchor" href="#attack-vectors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Attack vectors&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="injection-custom-root-ca" tabindex="-1">Injection (custom root CA) <a class="header-anchor" href="#injection-custom-root-ca" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Injection (custom root CA)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The most common attack against HTTPS traffic in mobile applications, and the easiest to execute: it requires no network-level privileges and no infrastructure compromise, just physical or remote access to the device.</p><p>The attack exploits a basic property of PKI: the OS trusts any certificate signed by a CA in its root store. An attacker generates their own root certificate, installs it into the device&#39;s trusted store, and from that point can issue certificates for any domain that the OS treats as valid. The device connects to the attacker&#39;s proxy, which presents a &quot;valid&quot; certificate signed by the installed CA to the client while impersonating a regular client to the real server. All traffic through the channel — headers, tokens, bodies — is visible in plaintext. Tools like Charles Proxy, Burp Suite, and mitmproxy automate the whole process (CA generation, installation, live interception) in minutes.</p><p>Prior to Android 7.0 (API 24), apps trusted user-installed CAs by default. Since Android 7.0, apps no longer trust user CAs unless the developer explicitly allows it in <code>network_security_config.xml</code> — closing the attack surface for ordinary users, but not for rooted devices, where a user certificate can still be moved to the system store.</p><h3 id="certificate-authority-compromise-rogue-ca" tabindex="-1">Certificate Authority compromise (rogue CA) <a class="header-anchor" href="#certificate-authority-compromise-rogue-ca" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Certificate Authority compromise (rogue CA)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Unlike injection, this requires no access to the victim&#39;s device at all. Compromising a CA lets an attacker issue a cryptographically valid certificate for any domain — one that every device trusting that CA will accept.</p><p>The <strong>DigiNotar incident (2011)</strong> remains the clearest example: the Dutch CA was breached, and attackers issued over 500 fraudulent certificates, including a wildcard for <code>*.google.com</code>, used to intercept HTTPS traffic to Google services for an estimated 300,000 users, predominantly in Iran. Every major browser and OS revoked trust in DigiNotar once discovered; the company went bankrupt within weeks. (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DigiNotar" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">more detail</a>)</p><p>A less obvious surface is Domain Control Validation (DCV) itself — the process a CA uses to confirm a requester controls a domain. One historical method relied on an email address listed in a domain&#39;s WHOIS record, which can be outdated, compromised, or publicly accessible. In December 2024, SSL.com discontinued this method on exactly those grounds. (<a href="https://www.securityweek.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">more detail</a>)</p><h3 id="mitm-via-rogue-access-point-evil-twin" tabindex="-1">MITM via rogue access point (Evil Twin) <a class="header-anchor" href="#mitm-via-rogue-access-point-evil-twin" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;MITM via rogue access point (Evil Twin)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An Evil Twin attack needs only a laptop and a Wi-Fi adapter in a public place. Most devices auto-connect to known SSIDs; an attacker broadcasts an access point with the same name as a legitimate network (<code>Airport_Free_WiFi</code>, <code>Starbucks</code>) at a stronger signal, and devices in range reconnect automatically. From there, all traffic flows through the attacker.</p><p>Evil Twin alone only achieves network-layer interception — decrypting HTTPS still requires combining it with a rogue CA or a flaw in the app&#39;s certificate validation. In 2024, Australian Federal Police charged a man who ran Evil Twin attacks on domestic flights and at airports, harvesting credentials from passengers who opened login pages in a browser — mobile apps with proper certificate validation were unaffected. (<a href="https://www.afp.gov.au/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">more detail</a>)</p><p>Why this matters for mobile apps specifically: users have no visibility into which access point their traffic is routing through, and apps run in the background continuously (sync, analytics, push). Even a brief connection to a rogue AP can leak an authorization token if the app validates certificate <em>identity</em> rather than key identity.</p><h3 id="bgp-hijacking-fraudulent-certificate" tabindex="-1">BGP hijacking + fraudulent certificate <a class="header-anchor" href="#bgp-hijacking-fraudulent-certificate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;BGP hijacking + fraudulent certificate&quot;">​</a></h3><p>BGP hijacking operates at the level of global internet routing, needs no physical proximity to the victim, and can affect every user of a service simultaneously. BGP has no built-in cryptographic verification of route announcements — an attacker with access to a BGP router can announce a shorter path to a target IP range, and a portion of global traffic starts routing through them.</p><p>Interception alone is useless against encrypted connections. This is where ACME&#39;s <code>http-01</code> domain validation comes in: the CA sends an HTTP request to the domain&#39;s IP and expects a specific token back. If the attacker has hijacked the route to that IP, the request reaches them, they return the correct token, and receive a valid certificate for a domain they don&#39;t own.</p><p>The <strong>Amazon Route 53 incident (2018)</strong>: attackers hijacked BGP routes to Amazon&#39;s Route 53 DNS servers for about two hours, targeting <code>myetherwallet.com</code>. They served a spoofed IP with a valid SSL certificate obtained in advance — users saw the correct address and a closed padlock. About $17 million in cryptocurrency was stolen. (<a href="https://www.internetsociety.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">more detail</a>) From the client&#39;s perspective everything looks legitimate — DNS resolves correctly, the certificate is valid, the handshake succeeds. The only reliable client-side defense is knowing in advance which specific public key the server should present, and rejecting anything else.</p><h3 id="ssl-stripping" tabindex="-1">SSL stripping <a class="header-anchor" href="#ssl-stripping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;SSL stripping&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Where the attacks above substitute or forge certificates, SSL stripping removes encryption entirely so there&#39;s nothing to break. Publicly demonstrated by Moxie Marlinspike at Black Hat 2009 with the tool <code>sslstrip</code>: a user requests <code>example.com</code> without a scheme, the server redirects to HTTPS, and a MITM intercepts that redirect — establishing HTTPS to the real server itself while continuing to serve the client over plain HTTP.</p><p>Unlike browsers, mobile apps have no address bar, so a user can&#39;t see the scheme is wrong. Cleartext traffic was permitted by default prior to Android 9 (API 28); since then it&#39;s blocked by default, but developers frequently re-enable it in <code>network_security_config.xml</code> for debugging or legacy dependencies and forget to remove it before release. <code>WebView</code> is a separate surface: it inherits browser-engine behavior including following HTTP redirects, and is vulnerable in full if not explicitly configured to enforce HTTPS.</p><p>HSTS (HTTP Strict Transport Security) partially mitigates this — once a client has seen an HSTS header over a secure connection, it upgrades subsequent requests automatically — but only if the <em>first</em> connection was secure (the Trust On First Use gap). Certificate Transparency doesn&#39;t help here either: there&#39;s no certificate at all in an SSL-stripped connection, so there&#39;s nothing to log.</p><h2 id="why-static-pinning-isn-t-enough" tabindex="-1">Why static pinning isn&#39;t enough <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-static-pinning-isn-t-enough" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why static pinning isn&#39;t enough&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Classic SSL pinning hardcodes the SHA-256 hash of the server&#39;s public key directly into the app binary. That closes every attack vector above — the client no longer trusts the CA hierarchy, it trusts one specific key. But it introduces an operational problem: any certificate rotation on the server requires a new app release, a store review, and waiting for users to update. During an emergency key rotation, part of the user base loses access to the service for days.</p><p>Workarounds — pinning an intermediate CA instead of the leaf certificate, or bundling multiple pins — either weaken the security guarantee or require tight coordination across teams. Revocation mechanisms (CRL, OCSP) don&#39;t help either: they&#39;re cached for hours and fail open when unavailable.</p><p>The alternative — covered in <a href="./architecture">Architecture</a> — is to move the pin registry outside the binary entirely: the app fetches the current list of trusted keys from a dedicated endpoint, the server updates that registry in real time, and the only thing that stays hardcoded is the public key needed to verify the registry&#39;s signature.</p>',28)])])}const f=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
